// SL-274 review r1 finding P1: two modules may export DISTINCT C names from one
// Saw name.
//
// `@export` decides the EMITTED C symbol and the linkage. It does NOT decide the
// compiler's internal identity — `_declare_function` has always kept the two
// apart, `func_name` being the `self.functions` lookup key and `llvm_name` the
// requested C symbol — so a declaration keeps its `<name>$m$<module>` identity
// everywhere the compiler names it and is emitted under the C name.
//
// The first draft of SL-274 exempted an `@export`ed function from the mangling on
// the reasoning that its LLVM name was the export name anyway. That was wrong
// about which of the two the exemption reached: it returned an EMPTY symbol base,
// so both `value`s here shared the lookup key `value`, the second overwrote the
// first, and the first's body was never emitted. The `@llvm.used` keep-root then
// named a declaration with no definition:
//
//     Linking failed: Undefined symbols for architecture arm64:
//       "_first_value", referenced from:
//           <initial-undefines>
//
// Both halves are checked here: the SAW calls (which go through the module-
// qualified identity) and the C calls back in through `extern "C"` (which go
// through the export names). The two must agree about which body is which.
//
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: saw 11 22
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: c 11 22

extern "C" {
    func first_value() -> Int
    func second_value() -> Int
}

module first {
    @export("first_value")
    public func value() -> Int { 11 }
}

module second {
    @export("second_value")
    public func value() -> Int { 22 }
}

func main() {
    print("saw {first.value()} {second.value()}")
    print("c {first_value()} {second_value()}")
}
